            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0338" n="331" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg331"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9183: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-05T18:00:00+08:00">August 5, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 5—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>19. I have been reliably informed that during the first 4 days of August 14,000
                    troops, 10 heavy howitzers, twenty-four 75-millimeter field pieces, 4 tanks, 25
                    armored cars and huge quantities of military supplies have been brought to
                    Tangku by the Japanese. Nearly all of the troops came by train but a few troops
                    and quantities of supplies were brought by ship. These troops and supplies are
                    being transferred to Tientsin and Peiping but it [is] not possible to check on
                    them here at the present time. No activity on the part of Japanese airplanes
                    here today as Tientsin airfields are too wet for bombers to take off and low
                    heavy clouds make airplane reconnaissance work impossible.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caldwell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
